  {section %title=Introduction
    {section %title=SMP complements
{req %id=simph.smpex.schedev.1
The scheduler shall emit an event named {kw Scheduler_PreEventExecute} before
executing the next scheduled entry point.
}
{req %id=simph.smpex.schedev.2
The scheduler shall emit an event named {kw Scheduler_PostEventExecute} just
after the execution of each schduled entry point is completed. 
}
    }
  }
  {section %title=Design
    {section %title=Mandatory services bundle
The kernel library include an implementation for each of the mandatory SMP services:
  - Logger
  - Scheduler
  - Timekeeper
  - Resolver
  - TimeKeeper

However those service shall be replaceable, meaning a user may substitute anyone
with its own implementation using the {kw AddService} method. To do so, the
simulator Publish process, start with a preliminary added service scan to find
the eventually provided substitutes, then create the default implementation
instance for each service not yet provided. From this step, the simulator is
configured to let service accessors (GetScheduler, GetTimeKeeper, GetResolver,
etc) to return the right service instance, the explicitly provided one or the
default created. 
    }
    {section %title=Path parsing
The bundled resolver parses the pathes to be resolved according the following state diagram.
{fig %src=resolver_path_parse_state.png %title=Pathp arsing state diagram}
{#
@startuml resolver_path_parse_state.png
[*] --> Sep: /
[*] --> Self: .
[*] --> Name
Sep --> Self: .
Sep --> Sep: /
Sep --> Name: name.append()
Self --> Parent: .
Self --> Sep: /
Parent --> Sep: / c=c.GetParent()
Parent --> [*]: \0 c=c.GetParent()
Parent --> [*]: c=null
Name --> Sep: / c=c.GetChild(name)
Name --> Name: name.append()
Name --> DotSep: . c=c.GetChild()
Name --> [*]: \0 c=c.GetChild(name)
Name --> Index: [ c=c.GetChild(name); name="["
Index --> Index: 0-9 name.append()
Index --> EndIndex: ] name.append(); c.GetChild(name)
EndIndex --> Sep: /
EndIndex --> [*]: \0
EndIndex --> [*]: c=null
DotSep --> Name: a-zA-Z_
DotSep --> [*]: c=null
@enduml
#}
    }
    {section %title=Scheduler / Time Keeper coordination
The scheduler / time keeper coordination relies on a specific event that is not
defined in the SMP standard: {kw Scheduler_PreEventExecute} shall be registered
in the event manager sometime during the simulator building phase. Then the
follwing policy shall be applied to let the time keeper and scheduler fulfil
each its own duty as defined in the SMP standard while being able to remain
consistent with each other:
  - the time keeper subscribes the {kw Scheduler_PreEventExecute} to trigger its
    own simulation time update process according the next scheduled event 
    simulation time.
  - the scheduler shall emit the {kw Scheduler_PreEventExecute} before running
    each scheduled entry point.  

{fig %src=SchedTimeKeeperEvent_seq.png %title=Scheduler / Time keeper coordination}
{#
@startuml SchedTimeKeeperEvent_seq.png
participant Scheduler
participant EventManager
participant TimeKeeper

group simulator building phase
TimeKeeper -> EventManager++: Subscribe(Scheduler_PreEventExecute, tickEntryPoint)
return
end

loop scheduler thread
Scheduler -> EventManager++: Emit(Scheduler_PreEventExecute)
EventManager -> TimeKeeper++: tickEntryPoint()
TimeKeeper -> Scheduler++: GetNextEventSimulatinoTime()
return scheduledEventTime
alt scheduledEventTime > current simulation time
TimeKeeper -> TimeKeeper: update simulation time
return
end
return
return

group scheduled entry point execution
end
Scheduler -> EventManager++: Emit(Scheduler_PostEventExecute)
return
end
@enduml
#}

Any alternate scheduler or time keeper service implementation may be used as
soon the {kw Scheduler_PreEventExecute} event is handled appling the policy
defined above in this section. 
    }
    {section %title=Simulation start / stop control
According to the SMP standard, the simulator shall emit events when starting and
stopping the simulation:
  - on simulation start request, if the current state allows it:
    - first {kw SMP_LeaveStandby}
    - then {kw SMP_EnterExecuting}
  - on simulation stop request, if current state allows it:
    - first {kw SMP_LeaveExecuting}
    - then {kw SMP_EnterStandby}

On start request, the simulator preforms the follwing actions:
  - schedule a start entry point immediately (to be first to execute).
  - emit the {kw SMP_LeaveStandby} event.
  - emit the {kw SMP_EnterExecuting} event.
  - wait for the start process completion.

The start entry point shall:
  - save the current thread identifier. Since it is executed by the scheduler 
    thread, this will be used on stop to detect the Hold method is called by
    a scheduled entry point, or externally.
  - notify the Start caller thread to release it.

The Simulator's Hold method shall stop the simulation according its boolean
argument:
  - when true: stop shall be immediate, this as soon the currently executed
    scheduled entry point is completed.
  - when false: stop shall be delayed when all entry point scheduled for the 
    current simulation time are completed. 

To do so, the simulator on Hold request, according the boolean
argument, schedules its own stop processing entry point:
  - immediatly (being the first entry point in the schedule list),
    when argument is true.
  - at simulation time now (being the last for the current simulation time in
    the schedule list), when the Hold argument is false.

Once the stop entry point is scheduled, the Hold method shall wait until the stop
entry point is completed (if not triggered by the scheduler thread itself),
then it emits the {kw SMP_EnterStandby} event.

The stop processing entry point shall emit the {kw SMP_LeaveExecuting} event, 
then it shall release the Hold caller thread and the simulator shall then switch
to standby state emitting the related event.

The scheduler shall:
  - subscribe to the {kw SMP_EnterExecuting} event to start its own scheduling
    thread.
  - subscribe to the {kw SMP_LeaveExecuting} event to change its running status
    and end itw own scheduling thread once the currently executing entry point
    is completed.

{fig %src=SimStartStop_seq.png %title=Scheduler / Time keeper coordination}
{#
@startuml SimStartStop_seq.png
participant Simulator
participant EventManager
participant Scheduler
Scheduler -> EventManager++: Subscribe(SMP_EnterExecuting, Scheduler::startEP)
return
Scheduler -> EventManager++: Subscribe(SMP_LeaveExecuting, Scheduler::stopEP)
return 
[--> Simulator++: Start
Simulator -> Scheduler++: AddImmediateEvent(Simulator::startEP)
return
Simulator -> EventManager++: Emit(SMP_LeaveStandby)
return
Simulator -> EventManager++: Emit(SMP_EnterExecuting)
EventManager -> Scheduler++: startEP
Scheduler -> SchedulerThread**: create
return
return
SchedulerThread -[#red]> Simulator++: startEP
return
return

[--> Simulator++: Hold(immediate)
Simulator -> Scheduler++: immediate?AddImmediat:AddSimulationTime(Simulator::stopEP)
return
SchedulerThread -[#red]> Simulator++: stopEP
 Simulator -[#red]> EventManager++: Emit(SMP_LeaveExecuting)
  EventManager -[#red]> Scheduler++: stopEP
  return
 return
 Simulator -[#red]> EventManager++: Emit(SMP_EnterStandby) 
 return
return
Scheduler -> SchedulerThread!!
return
@enduml
#}
    }
  }
}
